### <a id='mtls'></a> mTLS

To configure the exporter to use an mTLS connection to the remote OTLP endpoint, you can specify [additional TLS
configuration](https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector/blob/main/config/configtls/README.md).

```
otlp_grpc:
  endpoint: 203.0.113.10:4317
  tls:
    cert_pem: |
      PEM_ENCODED_CERTIFICATE
    key_pem: |
      PEM_ENCODED_PRIVATE_KEY
    ca_pem: |
      PEM_ENCODED_CERTIFICATE
```

### <a id='multiple-exporters'></a> Multiple exporters

The OTel Collector supports defining multiple exporters, each with its own configuration. For example, you can define two OTLP gRPC exporters that egress metrics to different destinations.

```
otlp_grpc:
  endpoint: 203.0.113.10:4317
otlp_grpc/another:
  endpoint: 203.0.113.11:4317
```

## <a id='metric-receiver-config'></a> Receiver configuration

The release always supplies the managed `otlp/cf-internal-local` receiver for telemetry from the Loggregator Forwarder Agent. Its definition comes from the BOSH job's `ingress.grpc.*` properties and overrides any operator definition with the same name.

To use custom receivers, define them in `config.receivers` and reference them in the appropriate `config.service.pipelines` receiver lists.

The BOSH job property `ingress.pipelines` controls how the managed receiver is wired:

| `ingress.pipelines` | Receiver definitions | Pipeline receiver lists |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Unset (default) | Replaced with only `otlp/cf-internal-local` | All replaced with `[otlp/cf-internal-local]` |
| `[]` | Custom definitions preserved; managed receiver supplied | Unchanged; wire all receivers manually |
| Nonempty list | Custom definitions preserved; managed receiver supplied | Only selected pipelines' lists replaced with `[otlp/cf-internal-local]`; other lists unchanged |

A nonempty list must contain exact configured pipeline names, for example `[metrics/inbound]`. Selected receiver lists are **replaced**, not extended. To combine managed and custom receivers in the same pipeline, use `[]` and explicitly list both receivers, as in the [custom receiver example](#custom-receiver-config).

Only the default, unset mode adds `nop` pipelines for missing traces, metrics, or logs. Explicit modes (`[]` or a nonempty list) add no `nop` fallbacks. Operators must configure pipelines to handle unwanted signals, for example with a `nop` exporter, or disable emission of those signals at their sources.

<p class="note">
<span class="note__title"><strong>Migration warning</strong></span>
Default behavior remains compatible. Before switching to an explicit mode, remove obsolete placeholder receiver definitions such as an empty <code>otlp/placeholder</code>. Explicit modes preserve these definitions instead of discarding them, and invalid definitions can prevent the Collector from starting.
</p>

See the [deployment instructions](#deploying) for release requirements and [Configuring custom receiver wiring in CF Deployment](#deploying-custom-receivers) for the supplementary BOSH operations file.

### <a id='included-receivers'></a> Included receivers
| Receiver | Stability
| :--- | :--- |
| `otlpreceiver` | stable |
| `filelogreceiver` | beta |

## <a id='grpc-compression'></a> gRPC compression

You can configure the type of compression used for OTLP gRPC exporters or receivers. The OTLP gRPC exporter uses `gzip` compression
by default. To reduce CPU usage of the OTel Collector (increasing the size of the payload) you can optionally configure
the OTLP gRPC exporter to use `snappy` compression. Validate that the server you are sending metrics to
support `snappy` compression before making this change.

```
otlp_grpc:
  endpoint: 203.0.113.10:4317
  compression: snappy
```

## <a id='metric-processor-config'></a> Processor configuration

Processors are used at various stages of a pipeline to pre-processes data before it is exported. By default, no processors are enabled. The order of the processors in a pipeline determines the order of the processing operations that the Collector applies to the signal.

Refer to the individual processor [documentation](https://opentelemetry.io/docs/collector/configuration/#processors) for more information.

Currently, the <%= vars.app_runtime_abbr %> distribution of the OpenTelemetry Collector supports the following processors out of the box: `batch`, `memorylimiter`. You can configure processors using the `processors` section of the Collector configuration file.

```
processors:
  batch:
```

### <a id='included-processors'></a> Included processors
| Processor | Stability |
| :--- | :--- |
| `batchprocessor` | stable |
| `memorylimiterprocessor` | stable |
| `transformprocessor` | beta |
| `filterprocessor` | alpha |

## <a id='authentication'></a> Authentication

The <%= vars.app_runtime_abbr %> distribution of the OpenTelemetry Collector does not currently include any authenticator extensions. Please let us know in the [Cloud Foundry Slack](#logging-and-metrics) `#logging-and-metrics` channel if there are authenticator extensions you would find useful.

## <a id='available-exporters'></a> Reviewing the available components

The <%= vars.app_runtime_abbr %> distribution of the OpenTelemetry Collector ships with a number of components. To see
the components that are available for use, run the following command on a VM that has otel-collector deployed.

```
/var/vcap/packages/otel-collector/otel-collector components
```

## <a id='service'></a> Service section

The  `service`  section is used to configure what components are enabled in the Collector based on the configuration found in the receivers, processors, exporters, and extensions sections. If a component is configured, but not defined referenced the  `service`  section, then it’s not enabled.

The service section consists of two subsections:

* [Extensions](#extensions)
* [Pipelines](#pipelines)

### <a id='extensions'></a> Extensions

You can enable desired extensions using `extensions` subsections:

```
service:
  extensions: [pprof]
```

#### Included Extensions

### <a id='included-extensions'></a> Included extensions
| Extension | Stability |
| :--- | :--- |
| `opampextension` | alpha |
| `pprofextension` | beta |

### <a id='pipelines'></a> Pipelines

A pipeline is a set of components used to process a signal. Before including a receiver, processor, or exporter in a pipeline, make sure to define its configuration in the appropriate section.

You can use the same receiver, processor, or exporter in more than one pipeline. When a processor is referenced in multiple pipelines, each pipeline gets a separate instance of the processor.

The following is a Collector configuration fragment, under the BOSH job's `config` property. The release supplies the managed receiver; define the referenced processors, exporters, and extensions separately. Note that the order of processors dictates the order in which data is processed:

```
service:
  extensions: [pprof]
  pipelines:
    traces:
      receivers: [otlp/cf-internal-local]
      processors: [batch]
      exporters: [otlp_grpc]
    metrics:
      receivers: [otlp/cf-internal-local]
      processors: [batch]
      exporters: [otlp_grpc]
```

## <a id='example-config'></a> Example config
Below is a complete input for the BOSH job's `config` property, not a complete BOSH properties map. Save this Collector configuration as the file supplied to `otel_collector_config` in the [deployment command](#deploying). For receiver wiring options, see [Receiver configuration](#metric-receiver-config).

```yaml
processors:
  batch:

exporters:
  otlp_grpc:
    endpoint: otelcol:4317

extensions:
  pprof:

service:
  extensions: [pprof]
  pipelines:
    traces:
      receivers: [otlp/cf-internal-local]
      processors: [batch]
      exporters: [otlp_grpc]
    metrics:
      receivers: [otlp/cf-internal-local]
      processors: [batch]
      exporters: [otlp_grpc]
```

### <a id='custom-receiver-config'></a> Custom filelog receiver example

This **BOSH job properties** example collects logs from files on the VM and combines them with logs from the managed receiver. Traces and metrics use only the managed receiver. For receiver wiring options, see [Receiver configuration](#metric-receiver-config). Only the contents of `config` are Collector configuration.

```yaml
ingress:
  pipelines: []
config:
  receivers:
    filelog/custom:
      include:
        - /var/vcap/sys/log/YOUR_JOB/*.log
      start_at: end
  processors:
    batch:
  exporters:
    otlp_grpc:
      endpoint: otelcol:4317
  service:
    pipelines:
      traces:
        receivers: [otlp/cf-internal-local]
        processors: [batch]
        exporters: [otlp_grpc]
      metrics:
        receivers: [otlp/cf-internal-local]
        processors: [batch]
        exporters: [otlp_grpc]
      logs:
        receivers: [otlp/cf-internal-local, filelog/custom]
        processors: [batch]
        exporters: [otlp_grpc]
```

Set `ingress.pipelines` using a supplementary operations file as shown in [Configuring custom receiver wiring in CF Deployment](#deploying-custom-receivers).

## <a id='deploying'></a> Deploying the OpenTelemetry Collector

CF Deployment provides [experimental operations files](https://github.com/cloudfoundry/cf-deployment/tree/main/operations/experimental)
that you can use to deploy the OTel Collector:

* [on Linux](https://github.com/cloudfoundry/cf-deployment/blob/main/operations/experimental/add-otel-collector.yml)
* [on Windows](https://github.com/cloudfoundry/cf-deployment/blob/main/operations/experimental/add-otel-collector-windows.yml)

Custom receiver wiring via `ingress.pipelines` is available in [otel-collector-release v0.11.13](https://github.com/cloudfoundry/otel-collector-release/releases/tag/v0.11.13) or later.

For example, to deploy a fresh CF Deployment with OTel Collector enabled, run:

```
bosh deploy cf-deployment.yml \
  --ops-file=operations/experimental/add-otel-collector.yml \
  --var-file=otel_collector_config=YOUR_COLLECTOR_CONFIG_FILE_PATH.yml \
  --var=system_domain=YOUR_SYSTEM_DOMAIN
```

The supplied file must contain the full Collector configuration, such as the [example config](#example-config). The `--var-file` option passes the file contents as a string, which the BOSH job template parses as YAML. The operations file places it under the job's `config` property and supplies the managed receiver's TLS certificates.

### <a id='deploying-custom-receivers'></a> Configuring custom receiver wiring in CF Deployment

When using a release that supports `ingress.pipelines`, save the following supplementary BOSH operations file as `custom-otel-ingress.yml` to enable manual receiver wiring on Linux:

```yaml
- type: replace
  path: /addons/name=otel-collector/jobs/name=otel-collector/properties/ingress/pipelines?
  value: []
```

Apply it with `--ops-file=custom-otel-ingress.yml` **after** the addon operations files. To select pipelines instead of manual wiring, replace `[]` with their exact names, for example `[metrics]`; each selected receiver list will be replaced, not extended.
